167
00:08:32,080 --> 00:08:33,600
Let me try to explain this using an example.

168
00:08:34,500 --> 00:08:46,280
If I have a size seven shoe and my brother gives me a Christmas present of size nine shoes, I say, Peter, these shoes are two sizes too big.

169
00:08:46,680 --> 00:08:46,840
Okay.

170
00:08:47,480 --> 00:08:50,040
That's the number two, two sizes, T-O-O, too big.

171
00:08:50,200 --> 00:08:52,440
That means they're too big for me by two sizes.

172
00:08:52,440 --> 00:08:53,440
Okay.

173
00:08:53,440 --> 00:08:54,440
Because the difference between seven and nine is two.

174
00:08:54,920 --> 00:08:55,920
Right.

175
00:08:55,920 --> 00:09:00,440
And if I have a size five shoe, I say, Peter, these shoes are two sizes too small.

176
00:09:00,440 --> 00:09:02,640
That means that five is two less than seven.

177
00:09:02,820 --> 00:09:04,560
So this is a very popular construction.

178
00:09:05,080 --> 00:09:14,560
So you're saying specifically how much bigger or how much smaller the shoes are in the case with the ring or shoes or anything that has a size.

179
00:09:15,280 --> 00:09:16,280
Exactly.

180
00:09:16,280 --> 00:09:18,240
But the funny thing is you can also exaggerate.

181
00:09:18,360 --> 00:09:21,240
You could say, oh, this sweater is so ugly.

182
00:09:21,240 --> 00:09:22,980
It's 10 sizes too big for me.

183
00:09:23,200 --> 00:09:24,380
It's not really 10 sizes.

184
00:09:24,640 --> 00:09:25,640
It's not really 10 sizes.

185
00:09:25,640 --> 00:09:26,640
Right.

186
00:09:26,640 --> 00:09:28,160
So, but you can use this construction, right?

187
00:09:28,160 --> 00:09:30,060
Using one size too big, etc.

188
00:09:30,400 --> 00:09:37,000
Even when you're buying something to the store, to the lady in the store, you can say, oh, I think the shoe is two sizes too big.

189
